<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willibrord</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 658–<a href="/years/739">739</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Willibrord</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willibrord was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> missionary from Northumbria, known as the Apostle of the Frisians. Arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> in 690 with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin of Herstal</a>, he was consecrated archbishop of the Frisians by Pope Sergius I in 695 and made Utrecht his see. He also founded the abbey of Echternach in present-day Luxembourg, where he died and is buried.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willibrord.jpg" alt="Willibrord" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georg_Sturm,_Willibrordus_predikt_het_Christendom_aan_de_Friezen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Willibrord, in Latin Villibrordus, was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> monk and missionary who became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Utrecht. Alcuin styled his father, Wilgils or Hilgis, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> of Northumbria. Wilgils, newly converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, entrusted his son as an oblate to Ripon Abbey and withdrew from the world to build a small oratory dedicated to Saint Andrew near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>. Willibrord grew up under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>, Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, and later joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedictines">Benedictines</a>. He spent the years between the ages of 20 and 32 at the Abbey of Rath Melsigi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-carlow">County Carlow</a> in Ireland, a centre of European learning, where he studied under Ecgberht of Ripon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ecgberht sent Willibrord and eleven companions to Christianise the pagan Frisians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> coast, at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-herstal">Pepin of Herstal</a>, the Austrasian mayor of the palace, who had nominal suzerainty over the region. Willibrord went twice to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Bede notes that he did not go as a pilgrim to the graves of the apostles but to seek the licence and blessing of Pope Sergius for preaching to the Gentiles. On 21 November 695, in the Church of Santa Cecilia in Trastevere, Sergius I gave him a pallium and consecrated him. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> to preach and found churches, among them a monastery at Utrecht, where he built his cathedral.</p><p>In 698 he established the Abbey of Echternach on the site of a Roman villa, given to him by Irmina of Oeren, Pepin's mother-in-law, who had sought his help when a plague threatened her convent at Horren. After Pepin's death in 714 the pagan Radbod, king of the Frisians, retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> in 716, burning churches and killing many missionaries, and Willibrord and his monks fled. After Radbod died in 719 Willibrord returned under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> and repaired the damage, assisted by Boniface.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Willibrord died on 7 November 739 at the age of 81 and, as he wished, was buried at Echternach. He was quickly canonised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> along his missionary routes were visited by people seeking cures for nervous diseases, particularly in children. A dancing procession is held at Echternach every year on Whit Tuesday, drawing thousands of participants and spectators, and he is often called the apostle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and Luxembourg. His feast day in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> is 7 November, except in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where it is 29 November.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Willibrord” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Heden II supported the Anglo-Saxon missionary <b>Willibrord</b>, who received estates at Arnstadt in 704, and a church was founded on the Marienberg at Würzburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-thuringia">Duchy of Thuringia</a></li><li><blockquote>King Redbad opposed the Christian mission of <b>Willibrord</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/frisian-kingdom">Frisian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Utrecht had been the seat of <b>Willibrord's</b> mission to the Frisians since the late 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-utrecht">Prince-Bishopric of Utrecht</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Northumbria. Died in Echternach, in what is now <a href="/countries/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.81,6.42&amp;year=710" aria-label="Echternach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4697 430 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Willibrord"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5147 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5147 700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.81,6.42&amp;year=710">What was here in 710 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
